# Evergreen docs describe the current code only

**Activation:** `alwaysApply: false` — when triggers in `agent-discipline-core.mdc` match, **Read this file before editing**. Triggered rules are mandatory, not optional.

## Scope

Evergreen pages are reference documentation for the code as it is at `HEAD`:

| Path | Notes |
|------|-------|
| `README.md` | Landing page; content trigger `readme-installation-sync.mdc` |
| `docs/index.md` | Docs map |
| `docs/tutorials/**`, `docs/how-to/**`, `docs/reference/**`, `docs/explanation/**` | Every quadrant page |
| `monitor/docs/how-to/**`, `monitor/docs/explanation/**`, `monitor/docs/reference/**` | Monitor quadrant pages (placement: `monitor-docs-structure-and-style.mdc` in the monitor tree) |
| `docs/using-the-website-as-a-researcher.md` | Core doc at its current path until it moves into the quadrant tree; content trigger `researcher-job-detail-doc-sync.mdc`. The testing pages (`docs/how-to/develop/run-tests.md`, `docs/reference/test-layout.md`, `docs/how-to/develop/run-sync-timedb-benchmarks.md`, `docs/how-to/develop/diagnose-update-metrics.md`) take content triggers from `testing-doc-sync.mdc` (`deploy-concurrency-doc-sync.mdc` and `design-document-sync.mdc` cover the quadrant pages that hold the deploy and design facts) |

Exempt:

- Records: `docs/history/**`, `docs/measurements/**`, `docs/plans/**` and the paper kits describe what was true at a date and are never rewritten to match new code (`docs-structure-and-style.mdc`).
- `docs/reference/release-notes.md` is evergreen and append-only: exempt **only** from the current-state wording rows below (it records one-time cutovers); the character, paste-safety and verified-claim rules still apply.

This rule governs **how** evergreen pages are written. Placement and moves: `docs-structure-and-style.mdc`. The sync rules govern **when** a code change must update a page.

## Don't / Do

| Don't | Do |
|-------|----|
| "Document history" / changelog tables, dated change rows | Nothing; `git log -- <file>` is the history |
| "Previously X, now Y", "no longer", "used to", "was changed to", "since 2026-11-02 X runs …" | "X runs …" (state the current behaviour) |
| Old defaults, removed options, removed tests, removed scripts or pages | Delete them; a one-time operator cutover goes in `docs/reference/release-notes.md` |
| "After the fix …", "this change makes …", commit or plan IDs explaining why text changed | Describe how the system behaves; put rationale in the commit message |
| Version numbers copied from memory | Versions read from the pin that sets them (Dockerfile `ARG`, `pyproject.toml`, `package.json`, `docker-compose.defaults.yaml`) |
| INI keys, defaults, script flags, test paths, log markers, service names written from memory | The exact spelling and value from code (`hpcperfstats/dbload/lib/conf_parser.py` `INI_OPTION_DEFAULTS`, `hpcperfstats.ini.example`, argparse/usage, `grep` for the log string) |
| Guessing when code is unclear | Leave the claim and ask the user |

Code that still reads an older on-disk format (for example legacy `.tar.gz` archives) is current behaviour: document it as what the code does today, not as history.

## Required actions when editing any of these files

1. Check every claim you add or touch against code before writing it. Cite nothing you have not verified.
2. Remove changelog / old-version text you find in the section you edit (same task).
3. Keep bash/sh fences paste-safe: no heredocs, no line over 68 characters.
4. Characters: ASCII plus three exceptions - the degree sign (U+00B0), the micro sign (U+00B5), and accented letters in proper names spelled as their owner spells them. Everything else is out: write `-` or ` - ` for dashes (never glue two words with a bare `-` where a dash was meant), `->` / `<->` for arrows, `x`, `>=`, `<=`, `...`, `~`, `section` for the section sign; no smart quotes, check marks, emoji or non-breaking spaces. Write code identifiers in backticks (`day_close`, `host_data`); headings may use plain words.
5. Run `hpcperfstats/tests/test_core_docs_drift_guard.py`; it fails on disallowed characters, changelog headings, old-version phrasing, bash/sh fence lines over 68 characters or with heredocs, INI keys missing from `INI_OPTION_DEFAULTS`, stated INI defaults that disagree with code, and repo paths in the testing doc that do not exist.

## Overlap

- `docs-structure-and-style.mdc` (and `monitor-docs-structure-and-style.mdc` in the monitor tree) — placement, records vs evergreen, templates, moves.
- `no-dead-code-or-stale-comments.mdc` — same principle for code comments.
- `feature-removal-doc-sweep.mdc` — removing a user-visible feature sweeps these docs in the same task.
- `readme-installation-sync.mdc`, `researcher-job-detail-doc-sync.mdc`, `deploy-concurrency-doc-sync.mdc`, `design-document-sync.mdc`, `testing-doc-sync.mdc` — per-doc content triggers.
